{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["The reason it mentions, after these commandments, <b>your animal you shall not mate kil'ayim</b>, is to warn after your being holy that you shall not do violence to a person like yourself, nor shall you do so to an animal, to alter the work of HaShem. Therefore it is written: 'My statutes you shall keep,' to keep every species that it not be mixed, species with species." "<b>Kil'ayim.</b> Two species. And I shall further explain the word kil'ayim. The reason for 'the field' and 'the garment' is to serve as a remembrance, for there are many commandments for a remembrance, like the festival of matzot and Sukkot and tzitzit and shofar and mezuzah and tefillin. And here I shall hint to you a sod. Know that the whole is very whole; therefore it is written of Abraham: 'and he kept My charge, My commandments, My statutes, and My teachings.'" "<b>Sha'atnez.</b> The translators said that it is three words or a quinquesected word, and its meaning is 'mixed,' and it is a word standing alone in Scripture."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:11:03.727388114Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}